NAME
====

**wattle-build** - build an image, executables, libraries or web programs

SYNOPSIS
========

**wattle build** ***img*** [**-l** _module_ | **--lib** _module_ ...] _source_
                 [_output_]

**wattle build** ***exe*** [**-r** _mode_ | **--release** _mode_]
                 [**-t** _triple_ | **--target** _triple_] [_name_]
**wattle build** ***lib*** [**-r** _mode_ | **--release** _mode_]
                 [**-t** _triple_ | **--target** _triple_] [_name_]
**wattle build** ***web*** [**-r** _mode_ | **--release** _mode_] [_name_]

DESCRIPTION
===========

**wattle build** creates two kinds of output. An image is made from a source
file and requires only the Wattle runtime. Executables, libraries and
WebAssembly programs are made from the exes and libs that a Wattle project
declares in its info file. A native executable, a native library and a web
program require the Zig toolchain.

**wattle build** can be invoked using ***wattle b***.

Subcommands
-----------

An image is made from a source file, not from a project's info file, and
requires nothing further.

- ***img***:
  Loads the module at _source_ and writes an image of
  its environment to _output_. If _output_ is omitted, it is _source_ with
  ``.wattle`` replaced by ``.wimage``. `wattle-run(1)` can be used to load
  the image later.

  - **-l**, **--lib** _module_:
    Imports _module_ into the environment before _source_ is loaded, as
    **wattle run** does. Several **-l** options run in the order given.

Executables, libraries and web programs are made from the declarations in the
project's info file. The generated files are in ``zig-out`` in the project
root, and a copied or fetched library is in ``.wattle``.

- ***exe***:
  Builds the exes of ``:type`` ``:native`` in the project's info file.
  The output is a standalone executable that contains the Wattle runtime, the
  compiled program, everything it imports and the native libraries whose
  ``:for`` names it. A source library that it imports must be placed by
  ***lib*** first. If _name_ is given, only that exe is built.

  - **-r**, **--release** _mode_:
    Optimises for ``safe``, ``fast`` or ``small``. The default is ``safe``.

  - **-t**, **--target** _triple_:
    Builds for _triple_, a Zig target such as ``x86_64-linux-musl``. The
    default is this machine.

- ***lib***:
  Places the libs in the project's info file on the module path, so that a
  bare import finds each by its name. A ``:source`` lib is copied to
  ``.wattle/<name>/``. A ``:remote`` lib is fetched into ``.wattle/.remote/``
  and then placed as the form its own info file declares. A ``:native`` lib is
  built as a native module in ``zig-out/lib``. If _name_ is given, only that
  lib is placed. Only this subcommand places libs: **wattle run** does not.

  - **-r**, **--release** _mode_:
    Optimises for ``safe``, ``fast`` or ``small``. The default is ``safe``.

  - **-t**, **--target** _triple_:
    Builds for _triple_, a Zig target such as ``x86_64-linux-musl``. The
    default is this machine.

- ***web***:
  Builds the exes of ``:type`` ``:web`` in the project's info file. Each
  is an image of the program, a Wattle runtime built for WebAssembly that loads
  an image, and the JavaScript that runs them in a browser. The ``:files`` of
  each doc whose ``:for`` names the exe are copied beside them. See
  `<Web programs>` for the files. If _name_ is given, only that exe is built.

  The runtime is built for ``wasm32-wasi``, so this subcommand takes no **-t**.

  - **-r**, **--release** _mode_:
    Optimises for ``safe``, ``fast`` or ``small``. The default is ``small``.

Zig toolchain
-------------

Building executables, native libraries and web programs requires:

1. ***zig*** to be on the user's `PATH`.
2. The Wattle source, which is included in Wattle releases and which is
   installed to ``<prefix>/share/wattle``.

No Zig build files are required. **wattle build** creates temporary
``build.zig`` and ``build.zig.zon`` files from the declarations in the
project's info file. It then uses these to build with ***zig***. Copying a
source library needs no Zig, and fetching a remote library needs ***git*** or
***curl*** and ***tar***.

The project's info file is ``info.edn`` in the current directory.

Declarations
------------

A project author declares what the project builds in three vectors of the
project's info file: ``:exes``, ``:libs`` and ``:docs``.

````
{:name "greeter"
 :exes [{:type :native
         :name "hello"
         :entry "main.wattle"}]
 :libs [{:type :native
         :name "greet"
         :root "greet.zig"
         :for ["hello"]}]}
````

Every exe and lib has a ``:type`` and a ``:name``, a string of letters,
digits, ``_`` and ``-``. A name is unique among the exes, and among the libs.
A doc has a ``:type`` and no ``:name``.

An exe has an ``:entry``, the file where ``main`` is defined, and one of these
types:

- ``:native``:
  A standalone executable that ***exe*** builds.

- ``:source``:
  A program that ``wattle apps install`` installs as source. **wattle build**
  does not build it. It also requires ``:files``, a vector of the paths to
  install. See `wattle-applications(7)`.

- ``:web``:
  A program for a browser that ***web*** builds.

A lib may have ``:for``, a vector of the names of the exes it is part of. A
native exe is built with each native lib whose ``:for`` names it, and a web exe
takes no lib. A lib has one of these types:

- ``:native``:
  A native module. It requires ``:root``, the Zig root file.

- ``:source``:
  Wattle source. It requires ``:files``, a vector of paths. Each is copied to
  the same path under ``.wattle/<name>/``, so ``:files ["util/"]`` puts
  ``util/init.wattle`` at ``.wattle/util/util/init.wattle``, which is imported
  as ``(import util/util)``.

- ``:remote``:
  A lib that is fetched from ``:url``, a Git repository or a URL ending in
  ``.tar`` or ``.tar.gz``. A Git repository requires ``:ref``, a branch or tag,
  and a tarball requires ``:hash``, ``sha256:`` and the tree hash of its files
  as ``wattle apps install`` records it under ``:rev``. The source must have
  an info file that declares a lib of the same ``:name``, as ``:native`` or
  ``:source``, and the lib takes that form. A lib is fetched again only when
  its ``:url``, ``:ref`` or ``:hash`` changes.

A doc requires ``:files`` and may have ``:for``. A ``:man`` doc is a set of
manual pages, each named with a period and its section, as in ``hello.1``. A
``:web`` doc is a set of files for a web exe.

The info file may not have ``:artifacts`` or ``:man``, and an exe may not have
``:libs``. **wattle build** names what replaces each.

Web programs
------------

Web programs are exes that are designed to run in a browser environment.
To do this, **wattle build** ***web*** creates four files in
``zig-out/web/<name>/``:

1. ``wattle-<hash>.wasm``, the runtime
2. ``<name>-<hash>.wimage``, the program
3. ``wasi-<hash>.js``, the WASI support shim
4. ``<name>.js``, an ES module that exports ``load`` and ``run``

Example programs are distributed with the Wattle source tree.

EXAMPLES
========

Compile a program into an image and run the image:

````
$ wattle build img hello.wattle hello.wimage
$ wattle run -i hello.wimage Alice
````

Build the native executables that the project's info file declares,
optimised for size:

````
$ wattle -p /usr/local build exe --release small
````

